Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Four Chairs Before the Snare

Story & Context: The opening track begins with the four vocal sections entering clockwise around the square. Tenor sings a blunt three-note fa-sol-la cell; alto answers after one empty beat; treble enters across the room; bass completes the circuit with a low open fifth. Ellis programs no snare during the first four rotations. Only one short kick appears beneath the bass answer, and even that arrives after the voice has established pitch.

When the halftime snare finally enters, it lands not on the expected third beat but immediately after a collective consonant at the end of the solmization phrase. The prepared piano answers with one muted dyad. The central fuging section then lets each voice begin the same melodic fragment at different times while the drums become progressively simpler.

The final thirty-two bars remove the electronic rhythm entirely. The choir repeats the opening circuit faster by human agreement alone, and the track ends on four overlapping vowels decaying into the wooden room.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Overhead documentary view of four sections seated around an empty square of scuffed floorboards, cream songbooks open to geometric note shapes, one compact drum machine outside the chairs, late sun cutting across black microphone cables. Lyrics begin primarily with raw fa-sol-la-mi solmization, then sparse text fragments: “four chairs / one breath / enter across / leave the middle empty.”

Track 2: Bass Note Arrives Before the Electricity

Story & Context: Leon proposes a rule for the second piece: every electronic low-frequency event must be earned by a natural bass-section note. He begins alone on a low modal tonic with no organ, no kick, and no sub. Thirty milliseconds after his second sustained note, a sine shadow appears beneath him and fades before his final consonant.

Ruth and June build narrow alto-treble clusters above the bass while Mara introduces a fuging line in five-beat phrase lengths. Ellis keeps the sequencer in 4/4, allowing the five-beat vocal cycle to drift through the kick pattern. Nora uses the pump organ only during collective breaths, swelling slightly after inhalation and disappearing once the voices return.

Halfway through, the electrical layers cut completely. Leon repeats the same low note unreinforced and it sounds smaller but more authoritative. When the sub returns sixteen bars later, it enters at half its former level. The track closes with the bass section stopping first, forcing the electronics to disappear before the upper voices finish.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close low-angle photograph of a bass singer holding an open shape-note book beside a small subwoofer with its power light barely visible, pump-organ bellows and folding-chair legs in the background, natural window light, plain documentary realism. Lyric material: “sound first / wire second / hold the root / electricity follows,” alternating with fa-mi-sol-fa syllables.

Track 3: Page Turn in the Empty Center

Story & Context: The EP closer is built around the smallest sound in the room: a coordinated page turn. Every singer begins on the same page, but no one may turn until Mara raises one hand. Ellis maps no sample of the paper. Instead, the actual live page turn becomes the cue for all programmed drums to stop for a full bar.

Before that moment, the track contains the densest broken-beat programming on the EP: clipped kicks, short ghost snares, asymmetric edits, and a restrained sub line that appears only beneath Leon’s lowest notes. The choir sings a fast fuging passage whose entrances spiral around the square while Nora punctuates section changes with deadened upright-piano strikes.

At the page-turn cue, six sheets move almost together. The drums vanish. What follows is forty-eight bars of unaccompanied singing in which the four sections gradually collapse from staggered entrances into one blunt homophonic chord sequence. Only after the final sung word does Nora press one pump-organ fifth.

The organ is allowed to decay fully. No beat returns. The final audible event is a single chair shifting in the empty square.

